  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 352 PM EDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nVisible satellite shows abundant cloud cover over the UP this \nafternoon, helping to hold temperatures down in the upper 60s to low \n70s across the area today as a mid level disturbance passes well \nsouth across southern Wisconsin and Lower Michigan. A band of light \nrain showers can be seen tracking east across the area on radar \nimagery, but this has produced no more than few hundredths of an \ninch of precipitation at any location thus far today and will remain \nlight as it continues eastward this evening. Chances for more \nmeaningful rainfall increase tonight as a warm front lifts into the \narea ahead of a surface low moving out of the Dakotas into the Upper \nMississippi River Valley, with PWATS surging above 1.75\" or close to \nthe max of nearby sounding climatology. Latest HREF guidance depicts \na 50-80% chance to receive a wetting rain of at least a quarter inch \nacross the western and southcentral UP through tonight through \nMonday afternoon, with closer to a 30-40% chance from Marquette into \nthe eastern UP. Could also see a few rumbles of thunder overnight \nwith this activity, but timing will help to limit any severe \npotential as CAPE will only be on the order of a few hundred J/kg.\n\nHighs will climb into the 80s on Monday afternoon as mid level warm \nadvection sends 850 mb tamps towards 20C. Some of the CAMS continue \nto depict a second round of showers and thunderstorms developing \nMonday afternoon as another disturbance moves in behind the departing \nwarm front. This convection would potentially have more to work with \nas the warm and moist environment yields increasing instability and \nbulk shear values increase to around 40 kt Monday afternoon. One \ncaveat is how much clearing will occur behind the initial round of \nconvection tonight into Monday, which could hinder how much \ndestabilization is ultimately realized. Regardless, SPC has pulled \nits Day 2 marginal severe risk northward into the southcentral UP \nfor Monday afternoon.\n\nThe forecast trends drier on Tuesday as Upper Michigan becomes \nsituated between low pressure over northern Ontario and high \npressure over the Tennessee Valley, which another day of highs in \nthe 80s expected. The ridge then reamplifies over the central CONUS \nfor the latter half of the week as the low over Ontario moves east \nto the Canadian Maritimes, placing the UP back into a northwest flow \nregime with temperature moderating into the 70s into next weekend. \nThis will favor generally drier weather through the end of the week, \nthough a few periods of showers can't be ruled out with any embedded \ndisturbances passing through the region.",
